 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: the printed record shows Cuba's stakes moving rather than simply growing — from a disordered neighbor whose wars injured American commerce (1870s–1898), to a guardianship that fused intervention rights, naval stations and Cuban prosperity (1901–1933), to sugar as the hinge of Cuban stability, U.S. exports and wartime supply (1933–1958), to a Soviet base and exporter of revolution (1959–1970), and finally to trade as political leverage and migration as a domestic problem (1977–1980); the set is about seven thousand documents the editors filed under Cuba in 130 volumes, 1849–1980. Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed documents came from the State Department's Central Decimal File (RG 59, Cuba files 837.00, 837.51, 837.61351 and U.S. trade 611.37) and the nineteenth-century despatch and legation-note microfilm before 1950, and afterward from the presidential libraries' national security files, CIA records and RG 59 lots such as 65D438 (Cuban Missile Crisis), 74D272 (Ball), 66D501 (Operation Mongoose), 62D24 and 62D418 (Inter-American subject files) and 62D1 (Policy Planning NSC records); the footnotes cite without printing chiefly decimal-file numbers, the same library files, lots 66D95 (NSC policy papers), 66D204 (heads-of-state correspondence) and 65D212 (sugar). What this request cannot get from FRUS: anything after 1980 (no Reagan-era document carries Cuba in its heading), a usable count of 'national security' (before the 1930s it almost never occurs and afterward it is mostly the name of the Council and no commoner in Cuba documents than in the corpus), and the commercial interests' own voice, which FRUS carries only through State Department correspondence.",
